                <pb facs="0199" n="169" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v24/pg169"/>

                <head>79. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On October 26, TASS announced that Marshal Georgi K. Zhukov had been replaced as
                    Minister of Defense by Marshal Rodion Ya. Malinovsky. The announcement provided
                    no other details.</p>
                <p>The initial appraisal by the Central Intelligence Agency of Zhukov’s removal was
                    in a memorandum from the Assistant Director of the Office of Current
                    Intelligence to the Deputy Director for Intelligence, dated October 26, in which
                    he concluded that Zhukov’s removal was likely intended to allow him to
                    concentrate more on political matters, that his political stature had not been
                    reduced, and <pb facs="0200" n="170" xml:id="pg170"/> that he probably would
                    continue as a leading member of the Soviet hierarchy. The memorandum concluded,
                    however, that “the possibility cannot be excluded at this time that Zhukov was
                    removed because of policy differences with Khrushchev or that Khrushchev moved
                    against the marshal to eliminate his most obvious contender for supreme power.”
                    (Eisenhower Library, Whitman File, International File—USSR 1956–1957)</p>
                <p>For Khrushchev’s recollection of the decision to remove Zhukov, see Khrushchev,
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Khrushchev Remembers,</hi> pages 11–18.</p>
            </div>
